## What runs (cron is opt-in)

The operator tick is `claude -p "/cs-operator"` (triage + cs-campaign-tick).
Turn it on from any session with **`/cs-cron`** (or by hand:
`cs cron install`; declared intent `{{ cron_schedule }}` — verify the live
crontab). Run **`cs config`** for the effective `cs_triage_mode` and the file
that declares it. The supplied cron wrapper keeps customer-send commands
denied independently of that mode and interactive permissions. Every tick
escalates on uncertainty. New campaigns are designed in a session too —
**`/cs-campaign`** builds the pack and the tick advances it.

**Pause:** `touch ~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/CS_PAUSE` prevents subsequent guarded
ticks and campaign sends; delete the file to resume. It does not terminate
running processes or stop independent engine work.

## What YOU do

1. **See what's ready** — open Gmail → **Drafts**: review and edit.
   Replies return to the mailbox and re-ingest automatically.
2. **Or open your agent** here (`codex`, `claude` or `opencode`). Start with
   `$cs-review` in Codex, `/cs-review` in Claude Code, or OpenCode's named
   `cs-review` skill. Deep-dive with `cs-account`, or just ask. `cs-help`
   shows the whole map with the invocation for your host.
3. **Send** — always your call, after review: use
   `cs draft-send <full-engine-draft-id>`, with the ID printed by
   `cs review --json`. This is the one contextual-send path; Gmail/Superhuman
   connector sends bypass the engine lifecycle and are not used here.

> The operator is **draft-first**. You are the send button.

### Load procedures into this session

Ask *"Load our company procedures and explain how we handle refunds"*, or invoke
`$cs-capabilities` in Codex, `/cs-capabilities` in Claude Code, or the named
`cs-capabilities` skill in OpenCode. It reads the authoritative `company/`
files into this conversation and reports missing or unwritten guidance.
Local procedure loading works offline, without MCP or ai-kit. It changes no
rules and publishes nothing; teaching or changing a standing rule uses
`cs-instructions`.

For questions about connected systems, the same skill checks engine capabilities
and its catalog when supported. Discovery explains operations without executing
them; an unreadable catalog leaves availability unknown. Host connectors are
separate from the engine's systems.

The CLI itself: `cs --help` is the reference (one line per verb,
`cs <verb> --help` for details); the verb map by risk class is in the
kernel README, [The `cs` CLI](https://github.com/malemi/cs-kernel#the-cs-cli).

## Setup / config

- Author `manifest.toml` (the single source of variance) and `company/*.md`
  (skill prose slots); everything else is stamped by `cs init` / `cs update`.
- `~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/.env` holds the secrets + real account uids
  (see [.env.example](.env.example)).
- Standing instructions (voice / policy) authored in `company/*.md`, then
  `cs instructions --commit` to compile and store them in the engine.
- `.venv` with the pinned kernel: `pip install -r requirements.txt`.
- `cs login` once — signs this clone in from the mrcall-desktop app's profile
  descriptor and stores the session at `~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/refresh_token-<uid>.json`.
  Every engine verb refuses with "not signed in" until you have.

## Safety (the NEVERs)

- Never sends without your review — draft-only by config **and** by permission.
- Never writes to anyone without their dossier; never cold-mails an address that
  already has a thread; never re-contacts within `DEDUP_DAYS` (default {{ dedup_days }}).
- Never touches another project's accounts.
- `~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/CS_PAUSE` blocks subsequent guarded ticks and campaign
  sends; running processes and engine work have separate controls.

